WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:11.673
Hinduism, the world's oldest major religion, developed on the Indian subcontinent in the Indus River valley.

2
00:00:12.174 --> 00:00:17.338
This religion sprang up about three thousand years ago and is still practiced by 80 percent of India's

3
00:00:17.378 --> 00:00:24.424
population. According to Hindu tradition, the course of people's lives and their place in society were determined at

4
00:00:24.484 --> 00:00:30.617
birth. What jobs they would perform, whom they would marry, what foods they would eat, and what customs

5
00:00:30.657 --> 00:00:36.361
they would follow were all already decided, based on their family's lineage and traditional occupations.

6
00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:42.725
As a result, society was divided into classes in a hierarchy called the "caste" system.

7
00:00:43.786 --> 00:00:49.810
Some historians think the system was introduced when the Aryans, a powerful nomadic tribe, migrated to the Indus

8
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.791
Valley around 1500 BC.

9
00:00:52.631 --> 00:00:58.095
When this group settled in the area, they added their gods to the existing religious beliefs and established

10
00:00:58.135 --> 00:00:59.056
the caste system.

11
00:00:59.956 --> 00:01:05.498
The creation of the castes is described in a poem in the Rigveda, an ancient and sacred Hindu

12
00:01:05.538 --> 00:01:11.840
book. It explains that a giant named Purusha sacrificed and dismembered himself to make the world.

13
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:18.702
The Brahman caste came from his head, the Kshatriya caste from his arms, the Vaishya caste from his

14
00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:21.262
stomach, and the Sudra caste from his feet.

15
00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:24.023
Each caste had different rights and duties.

16
00:01:24.824 --> 00:01:30.298
For society to function, every individual within the caste system had to do his or her job correctly.

17
00:01:31.418 --> 00:01:33.079
In the highest caste were the Brahmans.

18
00:01:33.739 --> 00:01:38.701
They were the priests and teachers of society and provided for the intellectual and spiritual needs of the

19
00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:44.023
community. Second in rank were the members of the Kshatriya caste—the warriors and rulers.

20
00:01:44.944 --> 00:01:47.025
They were the leaders and protectors of society.

21
00:01:47.725 --> 00:01:50.166
Next in the hierarchy was the Vaishya caste.

22
00:01:50.886 --> 00:01:54.768
People of this class were merchants and farmers and formed a sort of middle class.

23
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.341
At the bottom of the caste system were the Sudras—servants and workers.

24
00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:03.742
Their function in society was to work as laborers for the other castes.

25
00:02:04.443 --> 00:02:08.924
People of this class could not study religious texts or get a formal education.

26
00:02:09.764 --> 00:02:14.225
Even though most of their lives were spent in hard labor, the Sudras were taught not to struggle

27
00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:16.066
against their difficult role in society.

28
00:02:17.186 --> 00:02:22.548
There was, however, an even lower position in Hindu society, occupied by the "untouchables."

29
00:02:23.456 --> 00:02:27.659
These people were considered so low that they remained outside the caste system.

30
00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:34.884
Untouchables performed the "dirty" jobs that no one else wanted to perform, those dealing with blood, dirt, or

31
00:02:34.944 --> 00:02:40.027
death. It was believed they could contaminate the "clean" castes just by looking at them.

32
00:02:40.528 --> 00:02:44.851
As a result, strict rules were put in place to protect others from their "pollution."

33
00:02:45.731 --> 00:02:51.275
Untouchables could not remain on a path if someone from a clean caste approached; nor could they drink

34
00:02:51.355 --> 00:02:52.576
water from the same wells.

35
00:02:53.132 --> 00:02:57.935
They were also forbidden to wear shoes in the presence of members of the clean castes, to learn

36
00:02:57.975 --> 00:03:00.297
to read or write, or to worship in temples.

37
00:03:01.457 --> 00:03:06.861
Inequalities among the castes were, however, considered natural because they were decreed by the gods.

38
00:03:07.421 --> 00:03:12.364
It was thought that each person within the caste system had been assigned the position in society that

39
00:03:12.404 --> 00:03:13.425
he or she deserved.

40
00:03:14.025 --> 00:03:16.567
Movement up to a higher caste had to be earned.

41
00:03:17.087 --> 00:03:23.223
The belief in reincarnation—the concept that the spirit is reborn and returns to life after death again and

42
00:03:23.283 --> 00:03:25.504
again—is central to the Hindu way of life.

43
00:03:25.944 --> 00:03:29.886
The caste a person is born into is determined by that person's "karma."

44
00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:35.169
Karma is the sum of the good and bad deeds that an individual has committed throughout his or

45
00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:35.970
her lifetimes.

46
00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:42.253
It was assumed that people in the lower castes must have committed many evil deeds to be born

47
00:03:42.373 --> 00:03:43.334
into their positions.

48
00:03:43.854 --> 00:03:48.329
But hard work and acceptance of their status throughout life would allow people to come back in a

49
00:03:48.369 --> 00:03:50.150
higher caste in their next life.

50
00:03:50.711 --> 00:03:56.094
For instance, a warrior who performed many good deeds in his lifetime might become a priest in his

51
00:03:56.155 --> 00:03:56.695
next life.

52
00:03:57.475 --> 00:04:01.778
On the other hand, if someone tried to break free of his or her caste by changing professions,

53
00:04:02.219 --> 00:04:05.481
that person could be punished by being reborn into a lower caste.

54
00:04:06.782 --> 00:04:12.846
Over the centuries, many millions of India's underprivileged citizens, who lived on the fringes of society, had no

55
00:04:12.906 --> 00:04:15.568
hope of improving their lives or the lives of their children.

56
00:04:16.384 --> 00:04:22.727
Beginning in the 1930s, religious leader Mahatma Gandhi fought to change the patterns of oppression that had been

57
00:04:22.747 --> 00:04:24.507
a part of Indian culture for centuries.

58
00:04:25.168 --> 00:04:30.050
He gave untouchables a new name—Harijans, which means "children of God."

59
00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:36.812
In protest of the inequalities they faced, he stated, "I would far rather that Hinduism died than that

60
00:04:36.932 --> 00:04:38.073
untouchability lived."

61
00:04:39.093 --> 00:04:45.296
Discrimination based on caste and the concept of "untouchability" was outlawed in India's constitution of 1950.

62
00:04:46.708 --> 00:04:52.890
Since then, improved access to education and other resources has allowed many Harijans to learn to fight for

63
00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:56.931
their rights and take their places alongside workers of all different backgrounds.

64
00:04:57.691 --> 00:05:03.553
The boundaries of class are slowly disappearing, and some Harijans now hold positions of power and wealth.

65
00:05:04.273 --> 00:05:08.795
However, the caste system is still present in many areas of modern Indian society.

66
00:05:09.755 --> 00:05:16.054
Many villages are still segregated along caste lines, and some children are still divided according to caste in

67
00:05:16.074 --> 00:05:17.815
the classrooms of their primary schools.

68
00:05:18.875 --> 00:05:23.158
At one special time of the year, however, all caste distinctions are set aside.

69
00:05:23.658 --> 00:05:27.660
This occurs at the beginning of every spring during a festival called "Holi."

70
00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:35.324
Also known as the Festival of Colors, this Indian celebration can last for days and includes bonfires, music,

71
00:05:35.684 --> 00:05:36.805
dancing, and feasting.

72
00:05:37.845 --> 00:05:42.588
The festival is celebrated today as a way to recognize and embrace the diversity of all people.

73
00:05:43.168 --> 00:05:49.572
People soak themselves, friends, and strangers in colored water and paint themselves with herbal powders called gulal.

74
00:05:50.332 --> 00:05:56.556
All members of society mix freely, because the vibrant colors provide a disguise for everyone, and the strict

75
00:05:56.636 --> 00:05:59.797
code of conduct surrounding the castes is forgotten.

76
00:06:00.358 --> 00:06:06.461
This holiday, celebrated for thousands of years, is the one time when India's rigid customs are ignored.

77
00:06:07.202 --> 00:06:11.184
In India's modern society, many traditions and rules are now being questioned.

78
00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:14.770
Social and economic changes have begun and will continue.

79
00:06:15.651 --> 00:06:21.215
A booming middle class includes highly educated children who wish to become a part of a global culture.

80
00:06:21.795 --> 00:06:27.299
Although the caste system is still a significant part of Hindu life, many Hindus are now challenging its

81
00:06:27.359 --> 00:06:27.980
restrictions.
